Usage: hh_client check [OPTION]... [WWW-ROOT]

WWW-ROOT is assumed to be current directory if unspecified

Modes (mutually exclusive; choose one; default: --status):

  --cst-search                             Search the concrete syntax trees of files in the codebase for a given pattern
  --cst-search-files                       Run CST search on this set of files, rather than all the files in the codebase.
  --deps-out-at-pos-batch                  for each entry in input list get list of what it depends on [file:line:character list]
  --deps-in-at-pos-batch                   for each entry in input list get list of what depends on it [file:line:character list]
  --enforcement-at-pos-batch               show enforcement at multiple positions [file:line:character list]
  --file-dependents                        Given a list of filepaths, shows list of (possibly) dependent files
  --find-class-refs                        finds references of the provided class name
  --find-isolatable-clusters               find clusters of files that can be isolated from the codebase
  --find-refs                              finds references of the provided symbol; optionally specify the symbol kind like "Kind|Symbol" (looks for functions or methods if unspecified); valid kinds are Method, Property, Class_const, Typeconst, Function, Class, ExplicitClass, and GConst; use ExplicitClass instead of Class to exclude references via self/static/parent
  --find-my-tests                          return test files that reference the given symbols, using the version specified in the JSON config (default: staging)
  --find-my-tests-staging                  (deprecated, use --find-my-tests) alias for --find-my-tests
  --fun-deps-at-pos-batch                  for each entry in input list get list of function dependencies [file:line:character list]
  --go-to-impl-class                       goes to implementation of the provided class/trait/interface/etc. with the given name
  --go-to-impl-method                      goes to implementation of the provided method name
  --hack-to-notebook                       Convert a Hack file generated by `hh --notebook-to-hack` into a notebook. Pass hack file contents via stdin
  --ide-find-refs-by-symbol                similar to IDE_FIND_REFS, but takes a symbol name rather than position
  --ide-find-refs-by-symbol3               similar to FIND_REFS, but takes [action stream_file hints]
  --ide-go-to-impl-by-symbol               similar to IDE_GO_TO_IMPL, but takes a symbol name rather than position
  --ide-rename-by-symbol                   renames, but takes a Find_refs.action. Usage: hh_client <new name>|<comma_separated_action>
  --identify-function                      print the full function name at the position [line:character] of the text on stdin
  --identify                               identify the named symbol
  --in-memory-dep-table-size               number of entries in the in-memory dependency table
  --inheritance-ancestor-classes           prints a list of classes that this class extends
  --inheritance-ancestor-classes-batch     prints a list of classes that these classes extend
  --inheritance-ancestor-interfaces        prints a list of interfaces that this class implements
  --inheritance-ancestor-interfaces-batch  prints a list of interfaces that these classes implement
  --inheritance-ancestor-traits            prints a list of traits that this class uses
  --inheritance-ancestor-traits-batch      prints a list of traits that these classes use
  --inheritance-ancestors                  prints a list of all related classes or methods to the given class
  --inheritance-children                   prints a list of all related classes or methods to the given class
  --lint                                   lint the given list of files
  --lint-all                               find all occurrences of lint with the given error code
  --lint-stdin                             lint a file given on stdin; the filename should be the argument to this option
  --list-files                             list files with errors
  --lock-file                              show lock file name and exit
  --logname                                show log filename and exit
  --monitor-logname                        show monitor log filename and exit
  --client-logname                         show client log filename and exit
  --ide-logname                            show client ide log filename and exit
  --lsp-logname                            show client lsp log filename and exit
  --notebook-to-hack                       Convert notebook to a Hack file. Arg: notebook_number (example: "N12345"). Pass notebook in .ipynb format to stdin and a header (such as a copyright notice comment)
  --outline                                prints an outline of the text on stdin
  --outline-for-agents                     <file> outline the file, including line spans, to help agents efficiently navigate large files
  --package-lint                           Support for linting of __PackageOverride annotations
  --package-lint-full                      <target_file> <file1,file2,...> Package-aware find-refs lint
  --refactor                               rename a symbol, Usage: hh_client ["Class", "Function", "Method"] <Current Name> <New Name>
  --remove-dead-fixme                      remove dead HH_FIXME for specified error code (first do hh_client restart --no-load)
  --remove-dead-fixmes                     remove dead HH_FIXME for any error code < 5000 (first do hh_client restart --no-load)
  --remove-dead-unsafe-casts               remove dead UNSAFE_CASTS (first do hh_client restart --no-load)
  --rewrite-declarations                   Rewrite Hack source code allowed in notebooks to valid Hack.
For example in `function foo(): void {} function foo(): void{}` we
rewrite to the function names to something like `foo_1` and `foo_2`.
  --rewrite-lambda-parameters              rewrite lambdas in the files from the given list with suggested parameter types
  --save-naming                            Save the naming table to the given file. Returns the number of files and symbols written to disk.
  --search                                 --search this_is_just_to_check_liveness_of_hh_server
  --server-rage                            dumps internal state of hh_server
  --log-errors                             type check the given list of files and log their errors (use --log-to-file to specify output file)
  --stats                                  display some server statistics
  --status                                 show a human readable list of errors (default)
  --type-at-pos                            show type at a given position in file [file:line:character]
  --infer-dynamic                          infer types for dynamic locals [function_name or ClassName::method_name]
  --infer-dynamic-as-data                  infer types for dynamic locals as data [function_name or ClassName::method_name]
  --type-at-pos-batch                      show types at multiple positions [file:line:character list]
  --type-error-at-pos                      show type error at a given position in file [line:character]
  --is-subtype                             take a JSON list of subtype queries via stdin
  --tast-holes                             return all TAST Holes in a given file
  --tast-holes-batch                       return all TAST Holes for a set of files. Argument is a file containing a newline-separated list of files
  --validate-isolation                     <file> check whether the repo-relative paths listed in <file> could be a strict-isolation package; rebuilds decls in shared heaps, so use a server given over to this and restart it afterwards
  --verbose-on                             turn on verbose server log
  --verbose-off                            turn off verbose server log
  --version                                show version and exit

Options:

  --allow-non-opt-build                Override build mode check triggered by warn_on_non_opt_build .hhconfig option
  --autostart-server                   automatically start hh_server if it's not running (default: true)
  --config                             override arbitrary value from hh.conf and .hhconfig (format: <key>=<value>)
  --custom-hhi-path                    use custom hhi files
  --custom-telemetry-data Add          a custom column to all logged telemetry samples (format: <column>=<value>)
  --dump-config                        Output configuration
  --error-format <format>              Error formatting style (default: highlighted); valid formats: agent, extended, raw, context, highlighted, plain, plain_highlighted
  --isolation-movable                  ask whether the starting files can move as they stand, naming the files that block it; reads the dependency graph only, so a typecheck can still object
  --isolation-move-to                  <dir> move the directory named by --isolation-seed-dir into <dir>, under its own name, if it can move and the move introduces no errors; otherwise leave it where it was. Moves the files on disk and nothing else. The server should run with --config edenfs_file_watcher_enabled=true, or it may not see the move
  --isolation-grow                     grow a bigger cluster around each starting file, instead of reporting its smallest isolatable set
  --isolation-output                   <file> write clusters to <file> as JSON lines as they are found, and print only the summary
  --isolation-seed-framework           <class> start from every subclass of <class> — an entry-point family — instead of scanning for seeds
  --isolation-seed-list                <file> grow from the repo-relative paths listed in <file> instead of scanning for seeds
  --isolation-seed-dir                 <dir> grow from every Hack file under <dir>
  --isolation-max-dependents           <n> the most files that may depend on a candidate for it to be taken along with them
  --isolation-max-cluster-size         <n> stop growing a cluster once it reaches <n> files
  --isolation-allow-long-run           run even when the seed count says this will take hours
  --isolation-batch-size               <n> grow <n> clusters at a time; they are all held in memory together, so this sets how much memory the run needs, and a larger batch drops more overlapping clusters so it also changes how many are reported
  --isolation-max-seeds                <n> grow from at most <n> seeds
  --isolation-seed-offset              <n> skip the first <n> seeds; pair with --isolation-max-seeds to walk the seed list in chunks
  --force-dormant-start                If server is dormant, force start a new one instead of waiting for the next one to start up automatically (default: false)
  --from                               so we know who's calling hh_client - e.g. nuclide, vim, emacs, vscode
  --from-arc-diff                      (deprecated) equivalent to --from arc_diff
  --from-arc-land                      (deprecated) equivalent to --from arc_land
  --from-check-trunk                   (deprecated) equivalent to --from check_trunk
  --from-emacs                         (deprecated) equivalent to --from emacs
  --from-vim                           (deprecated) equivalent to --from vim
  --ignore-hh-version                  ignore hh_version check when loading saved states (default: false)
  --json                               output json for machine consumption (default: false)
  --jsonl                              output streaming jsonl (JSON Lines) for machine consumption (default: false)
  --max-errors                         Maximum number of errors to display
  --preexisting-warnings               show all preexisting warnings in typechecked files (default: false)
  --no-load                            start from a fresh state
  --prechecked                         override value of "prechecked_files" flag from hh.conf
  --no-prechecked                      override value of "prechecked_files" flag from hh.conf
  --with-mini-state                    Init with the given saved state instead of the one based on current repo version.
  --profile-log                        enable profile logging
  --reason                             reason for invoking hh, logged to telemetry
  --retries                            (deprecated) same as --timeout
  --retry-if-init                      (deprecated and ignored)
  --save-64bit                         save discovered 64-bit to the given directory
  --save-human-readable-64bit-dep-map  save map of 64bit hashes to names to files in the given directory
  --saved-state-ignore-hhconfig        ignore hhconfig hash when loading saved states (default: false)
  --show-spinner                       shows a spinner while awaiting the typechecker
  --single <path>                      Return errors in file with provided name (give '-' for stdin)
  --multi <path>                       Return errors for files read from the given file (one per line)
  --log-to-file <path>                 Write logged errors to specified file (use with --log-errors)
  --show-tast                          in combination with `--single`, output the TASTs of the file along with TAST hashes.
  --sort-results                       sort output for CST search.
  --stdin-name                         substitute stdin for contents of file with specified name
  --timeout                            set the timeout in seconds (default: no timeout)
  -Wall                                show all warnings
  -Wnone                               hide all warnings
  -W                                   show all warnings with a given code, e.g. -W 12001
  -Wno                                 hide all warnings with a given code, e.g. -Wno 12001
  -Wignore-files                       hide warnings in files matching a regexp
  -Wgenerated                          show warnings in generated files
  --watchman-debug-logging             Logs full Watchman requests and responses. This is very noisy
  --ultrahelp                          see all modes and options, including less frequently used ones
  -help                                Display this list of options
  --help                               Display this list of options
